# Elaichi conventions

Elaichi is an agent platform for companies. It connects the software a company
already runs on and lets agents work in those systems, inside the same
permissions the company already grants its people. One org-wide MCP endpoint,
authorized with OAuth, serves Claude, ChatGPT, Cursor and any other MCP client.
**MCP is how the product is delivered, not what the product is.**

## Skill routing

| The question is about | Load |
|---|---|
| What Elaichi is, where a screen lives, what a word means, what a plan includes | **elaichi** |
| Acting through an attached Elaichi MCP server | **elaichi-mcp** |
| Setting up Claude, ChatGPT, Cursor or another MCP client | **elaichi-clients** |
| Connecting accounts, connection health, transfer, offboarding, custom connectors | **elaichi-connections** |
| Toolboxes, templates, frozen params, delegation, synthetic tools | **elaichi-toolboxes** |
| Automations and workflows: triggers, steps, approvals, runs, sessions, webhooks | **elaichi-automations** |
| Collections, dashboards and public links, knowledge bases | **elaichi-data** |
| Roles, permissions, restrictions, access requests, audit, SSO, SCIM | **elaichi-governance** |
| Writing code against `api.elaichi.ai` | **elaichi-api** |

## URLs

| What | Where |
|---|---|
| API | `https://api.elaichi.ai` |
| MCP endpoint | `https://api.elaichi.ai/mcp` (`POST` only) |
| Console | `https://app.elaichi.ai` |
| Public dashboard link | `https://app.elaichi.ai/p/<token>` |
| Docs | `https://elaichi.ai/docs` |
| OpenAPI | `https://api.elaichi.ai/schema/openapi.json` (also `/schema/openapi.yml`, `/openapi.json`, `/openapi.yaml`) |
| Support | `support@elaichi.ai` |

Paths are unversioned. The API host is the same for every data region. If an
endpoint is not in the OpenAPI schema, it is internal and its URL is not a
stable contract.

## Authentication

```
Authorization: Bearer elch_{org_id}_{64 hex}
X-Organization-Id: org_…            # optional with a token, which already names an org
```

Create a token at **Settings → API tokens**. The raw value is shown **once**.

A token **has no scopes**. It acts as the person who created it, with their
live permissions — change their role and the token changes with them; remove
them and it stops working. It is bound to one organization: naming a different
one in `X-Organization-Id` is refused. Server-side only.

**An API token is the whole programmatic surface.** The console's own browser
sign-in is not an integration path, there is no supported way to obtain a
session outside the browser, and nothing should try to mint or replay one.

Some actions need a **person signed in at app.elaichi.ai**, and a token gets
`403` ("This action needs an interactive browser session"), not `428`: the
whole `/api-token` router, SCIM tokens, approving an MCP client, managing
connected apps, resolving access requests, deleting the organization. A
signed-in person doing a sensitive change may also be asked to confirm it is
them ("Confirm it's you"), and the API answers `428 step_up_required` until
they do. Either way, the right response is to send the person to the app.

Over MCP and in the Elaichi Agent there is no way to confirm it is you, so
these operations are refused there and must be done in the app: sending,
resending or revoking an invite, changing a member's roles, removing a member,
deleting a role or a team, and revoking an API token. Listing or renaming API
tokens works in the Elaichi Agent but is not offered over MCP at all.

MCP clients use neither: they sign in with OAuth and the person approves
scopes on Elaichi's own consent screen.

## The list envelope

Every endpoint returning an array:

```json
{ "result": [], "next_cursor": "opaque-or-null", "prev_cursor": "opaque-or-null" }
```

- `limit` — default **50**, max **200** (above is clamped; below 1 is an error).
- `cursor` — opaque, round-trip untouched.
- `q` — server-side search, up to 200 characters, applied before paging, on
  the routes that document it.

**Page until `next_cursor` is null.** Never search, filter, sort or count
client-side over a paginated list — you would be operating on the pages you
happen to hold. Counts come from the server.

The one exception is `/scim/v2/*`, which speaks RFC 7644's
`startIndex`/`count` because the spec requires it.

## Errors

```json
{ "error": { "message": "…", "code": "…", "details": { } } }
```

| Code | HTTP |
|---|---|
| `validation_error`, `bad_request`, `missing_organization_header` | 400 |
| `unauthorized` | 401 |
| `permission_required`, `forbidden` | 403 |
| `subscription_required` (no active Gold or Black plan), `feature_not_available` (not on this plan), `organization_blocked` (suspended by Elaichi support), `mfa_setup_required` (the org requires two-factor) | 403 |
| `automations_not_available`, `automations_disabled`, `automations_kill_switch` (the automation platform is not offered, turned off, or paused) | 403 |
| `not_found` | 404 |
| `conflict` | 409 |
| `payload_too_large` | 413 |
| `step_up_required` | 428 |
| `rate_limited` | 429 |
| `internal_error` | 500 |
| `platform_mcp_stopped`, `platform_connections_stopped`, `platform_signups_stopped` (paused for maintenance; retry in a few minutes) | 503 |
| `clove_timeout`, `saffron_timeout` (an internal service did not answer in time; retry) | 504 |

Many routes use a more specific code than these; branch on the ones you know
and show the message for the rest.

`permission_required` carries `details.required_permissions` — **any one** of
them suffices. Show `error.message` unchanged; it is written for a person and
already names the permission.

**A `404` may mean "not yours".** Resources you hold no grant on are concealed
rather than refused.

`X-Request-Id` is exposed on responses. Log it.

## Rate limits

| Surface | Limit |
|---|---|
| REST API | 600 requests / 60s per API token |
| MCP endpoint | 120 requests / 60s per OAuth access token |

`429` carries `Retry-After` in seconds. There is no public idempotency key.

## URL shape

| Kind | Pattern |
|---|---|
| CRUD | `GET/POST /resource`, `GET/PATCH/DELETE /resource/:id` |
| Action | `POST /resource/:id/<verb>` — `verify`, `reconnect`, `execute`, `transfer`, `test` |
| Nested collection | `/resource/:id/<collection>[/:childId]` |

Fields update through `PATCH /resource/:id` with an all-optional body; `null`
clears. **There are no attribute subpaths.** Unbounded child collections are
never embedded in a parent row — the row carries a count and a preview, and
the collection has its own paginated endpoint.

## Capability fields

Authorization is computed server-side and shipped as a field. **Read it; do
not re-derive it** from `owner_user_id` and a permission list.

`can_use` · `can_share` · `can_manage` · `can_transfer` · `can_revoke_share` ·
`can_see_shares`, plus a few resource-specific ones such as `can_reconnect`
and `can_delete`.

`can_manage` is the single name for "may mutate this resource's settings"
across every resource type — there is no `can_edit` or `can_update`.

`access_via` (`owner` / `direct` / `team` / `org`) reports how you reach a
resource, and is **omitted** when nothing does.

## Ids

TypeIDs: `{prefix}_{26 chars}`, a UUIDv7 in lowercase Crockford base32.
Opaque, but chronologically sortable within a prefix.

| Prefix | Entity | Prefix | Entity |
|---|---|---|---|
| `org` | organization | `conn` | connection |
| `usr` | user | `tbx` | toolbox |
| `mem` | org membership | `tbxe` | toolbox entry |
| `dom` | org domain | `tpl` | template |
| `team` | team | `tple` | template entry |
| `tmem` | team membership | `syn` | synthetic tool |
| `inv` | invite | `acl` | sharing grant |
| `role` | role | `rstr` | restriction |
| `mrol` | member role | `areq` | access request |
| `atok` | API token | `aud` | audit event |
| `sso` | SSO connection | `gmap` | group mapping |
| `stok` | SCIM token | `sgrp` | SCIM group |
| `ocli` | OAuth client | `ogrt` | OAuth grant |
| `ldst` | logging destination | `onfd` | notification destination |
| `pkey` | passkey | `skv` | toolbox or template skill version |
| `file` | stored tool file | `fupr` | file upload request |
| `crun` | one `run_code` execution | `req` | request id (logs and audit) |

The automation platform adds:

| Prefix | Entity | Prefix | Entity |
|---|---|---|---|
| `auto` | automation | `aver` | published automation version |
| `arun` | automation run | `ases` | automation session |
| `aapr` | approval | `awh` | webhook endpoint |
| `coll` | collection | `crow` | collection row |
| `cfld` | collection field | `dash` | dashboard |
| `pdl` | public dashboard link | `kbs` | knowledge base |
| `kdoc` | knowledge document | `bndl` | app (bundle) |
| `bver` | app version | `bins` | app install |
| `ulim` | usage limit | `pcap` | payment cap |

**Connectors are identified by slug, not by a TypeID.** A collection's extra
table is named by its key on the wire.

Three secrets embed an org id instead of being TypeIDs — `elch_…` (API token),
`einv_…` (invite), `escim_…` (SCIM token), all shaped
`{prefix}_{orgId}_{secret}`. The org id routes the request; the secret is only
ever compared as a hash. A public dashboard link's token starts `epd_`.

Dynamic toolbox ids are computed and read-only: `global:{userId}` (named
**All tools**) and `connection:{connectionId}`.

## MCP: namespaces and scopes

| Namespace | Cost |
|---|---|
| `elaichi__*` — administers Elaichi itself | One internal call. Cheap. |
| Connected tools, reached via `execute_tool` or `run_code` | Restriction check + credential fetch + live third-party call. Seconds, and the vendor's rate limit. |

`elaichi__toolbox__execute` and `elaichi__synthetic_tool__execute` cost the
second kind, because they run a connected tool. Operation `a.b` in Elaichi's
catalog is MCP tool `elaichi__a__b`.

**Connected tools are never listed in `tools/list`, however few there are.**
`search_tools` finds one; `execute_tool` runs it; `run_code` runs a small
JavaScript program that calls several. `search_tools` indexes connected tools
only — never an `elaichi__` operation. A synthetic tool appears in it only as
an entry of a toolbox you can reach; a standalone one is listed by
`elaichi__synthetic_tool__list` and run by `elaichi__synthetic_tool__execute`.
Ranking is **lexical**: query with concrete tool-ish words, not a sentence.

When the person reaches no connected tool at all, `search_tools` and
`execute_tool` are not listed. Clients that support MCP Apps also get
`show_card` and `show_object`, which only draw cards.

A tool reaching several accounts of one app takes a required `connection`
argument — one of its schema enum labels, copied exactly, up to 12 accounts;
an exact account name or `conn_…` id beyond that. **Never guess it. Ask.**

| Scope | Consent screen label | Covers |
|---|---|---|
| `mcp:read` | Read your organization's data | Reads, including sensitive ones like the audit log |
| `mcp:write` | Create and change data | Creates and changes; implies read |
| `mcp:destructive` | Delete data and remove access | Deletes, removals, revokes; implies read. Never pre-ticked |
| `mcp:tools` | Run your connected tools | Connected tools, from all of them or chosen toolboxes. A tool that deletes needs `mcp:destructive` too |
| `openid`, `email` | Confirm who you are, See your email address | Identity only; reach nothing in the organization |

A client that asks for no scope gets `mcp:read` only. A grant's scopes never
change after consent; adding one means connecting the client again.

## Two rules that hold everywhere

1. **Nothing in Elaichi takes a credential from a model.** No tool or
   operation accepts a password, API key or token as input, and none returns
   one. A flow that seems to need one really needs a connect link the person
   opens themselves.
2. **A refusal names what it wanted, and that is the end of it.** Relay the
   permission or scope and stop. Reaching the same data another way is the
   failure mode the whole layer exists to prevent.
